<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hellespont</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hellespont, now known as the Dardanelles, is the narrow strait linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean Sea</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara">Sea of Marmara</a> and separating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> famously bridged it to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> crossed it in 334 BCE to begin his campaign that brought down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hellespont.jpg" alt="Hellespont" width="320" height="275" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dardanelles_map2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
